# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONE BOX. LIVE. FOUR LEGS SUBMITTED AS FAST AS THE BROKER PERMITS.
#
# This is the LIVE variant of deploy/supervised-live-test.env.template. That file is the paper
# rehearsal posture (BOX_EXECUTION_MODE=paper_latency, BOX_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED=false) and you
# should run it FIRST, unchanged, until you have watched a full entry→hold→exit→flat cycle.
#
# This file differs from it in exactly four ways, and nothing else:
#   1. execution really is live,
#   2. the four-leg entry burst is allowed to overlap (concurrency 4 rather than 1),
#   3. the operator blocklist is documented as part of the pre-flight,
#   4. the comments state which knob actually provides the one-box guarantee.
#
# READ THE THREE WARNINGS BELOW BEFORE YOU COPY THIS FILE. Each is a thing the code does that the
# obvious reading of the variable name does not tell you.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# WARNING 1 — WHAT ACTUALLY GUARANTEES "ONLY ONE BOX"
#
# It is NOT `BOX_LIVE_MAX_OPEN_BOXES=1`. That guard reads `openBoxes`, which the engine only
# updates AFTER a position has been created (`positions.add()` then `syncManagerExposure()`), so
# two entry pipelines admitted in the same instant both see `openBoxes === 0` and both proceed.
# The session CYCLE budget does not close it either, because a cycle is counted as CONSUMED at
# establishment — again after the fills. Instrument reservations are per-CONTRACT, so two boxes on
# different underlyings share no key. `BOX_ONE_ACTIVE_BOX_PER_UNDERLYING` is, by construction,
# same-underlying only.
#
# The ONE mechanism that refuses a second concurrent entry on a DIFFERENT underlying is
# `BOX_SESSION_MAX_ENTRY_ATTEMPTS`, because `recordAttemptStarted()` is durable, serialised by a
# mutex, and re-evaluates the ceiling INSIDE the critical section. Its default is 0 = UNLIMITED.
#
# So `BOX_SESSION_MAX_ENTRY_ATTEMPTS=1` below is load-bearing. Do not raise it "to allow a retry":
# raising it to 2 permits two concurrent boxes, which is the exact thing this trial is bounding.
# If the first attempt fails and you want another, arm a NEW session deliberately — the engine
# refuses to re-arm while any consumed cycle still has live exposure, which is the check you want.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# WARNING 2 — "4 CONCURRENT LEGS" DOES NOT MEAN FOUR SIMULTANEOUS ORDERS
#
# Entry is HEDGE-FIRST, in two waves: the two BUY legs go first, and the two uncovered SELL legs
# are parked until every BUY has an attributed, single-use proof of fill (see
# entrySubmissionOrder.ts and the hedge coverage ledger). That is a safety property, not a
# performance setting, and concurrency cannot override it.
#
# Consequence: raising BOX_LIVE_ENTRY_SUBMIT_CONCURRENCY from 1 to 4 overlaps the two legs WITHIN
# a wave. It cannot overlap a SELL with the hedge it depends on. The repo's own benchmark shows
# concurrency 2 and concurrency 4 producing IDENTICAL four-leg completion distributions
# (docs/LATENCY_BENCHMARK.md §5) for precisely this reason.
#
# What you gain by setting 4 is the removal of the artificial serialisation of the two legs in each
# wave — worth having, and honest. What you do NOT gain is a one-round-trip atomic box.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# WARNING 3 — IF THE WEBSOCKET DROPS WHILE THE BOX IS OPEN, NO EXIT IS ATTEMPTED
#
# `BoxPositionMonitor.evaluatePosition` returns early on `!isFeedHealthy()`, and every exit's
# `stillWanted` predicate also requires a healthy feed. There is no REST/LTP fallback on the exit
# path and no "feed dead for N seconds ⇒ flatten" escalation. Monitoring continues and metrics keep
# updating, but nothing will try to close the position.
#
# Getting flat with a dead feed is therefore an OPERATOR action, and the routes for it are
# deliberately independent of the entry controls:
#     POST /api/box/live/cancel-working     (cancel every working box order)
#     POST /api/box/live/flatten            (reduce the exposure)
# Have both ready in a terminal before you arm. Do not rely on expiry-safety as a backstop — it is
# evaluated after the feed-health check, so a dead feed suppresses that too.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ── 1. EVERYTHING FROM THE REHEARSAL TEMPLATE ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Start from deploy/supervised-live-test.env.template and keep every value in it EXCEPT the ones
# restated below. It already carries the correct freshness gates, coherence limits, funding
# admission, capital caps and economic gates for a one-lot trial, and re-deriving them here would
# only create two files that can drift apart.


# ── 2. GO LIVE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both are required. BOX_EXECUTION_MODE=live without BOX_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED=true is refused at
# startup by design, and the mode is a CONSTRUCTION boundary — it cannot be changed from the UI at
# any privilege level, so this file plus a restart is the only way in.
BOX_EXECUTION_MODE=live
BOX_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED=true
ZERODHA_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED=true

# Mutually exclusive with live; restated so a copy-paste from the rehearsal file cannot re-enable
# them and be refused at boot with a message you then have to go and look up.
BOX_SHADOW_MODE_ENABLED=false
BOX_PAPER_EXECUTION_PROFILE=standard


# ── 3. THE ONE-BOX BOUND (see WARNING 1) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
BOX_SESSION_MAX_ENTRY_ATTEMPTS=1          # ⚠ THE guarantee. Not a nice-to-have.
BOX_SESSION_MAX_COMPLETED_TRADES=1        # one full ENTRY→HOLD→EXIT→FLAT lifecycle
BOX_LIVE_MAX_OPEN_BOXES=1                 # ⚠ 0 means NEVER OPEN here — the guard is `>=`
BOX_LIVE_MAX_CONCURRENT_EXECUTIONS=1      # transport serialisation; queues, does not refuse
BOX_MAX_CONCURRENT_PER_UNDERLYING=1
BOX_ONE_ACTIVE_BOX_PER_UNDERLYING=true    # code default is false — deliberately ON
BOX_LIVE_MAX_RESIDUAL_LEGS=0              # ⚠ comparison is STRICTLY `>`, so 0 tolerates none


# ── 4. THE FOUR-LEG BURST (see WARNING 2) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 4 = let both legs of a hedge wave travel together. The gap between successive placements is
# still a REAL rate-limit wait derived from Zerodha's published 10 orders/second, never zero and
# never below the 100 ms broker floor — see src/box/brokerPacing.ts. Leaving this at 1 is also a
# perfectly defensible choice for a first live run; it is slower and strictly no less safe.
BOX_LIVE_ENTRY_SUBMIT_CONCURRENCY=4
BOX_LIVE_BROKER_ORDER_MIN_INTERVAL_MS=0   # 0 = derive from the broker profile (110 ms, floor 100)
BOX_LIVE_BROKER_MIN_INTERVAL_MS=250       # status polls stay unhurried; separate bucket


# ── 5. NARROW THE UNIVERSE, THEN NARROW IT AGAIN ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Two independent controls, and they compose:
#   BOX_MAX_UNDERLYINGS caps HOW MANY names are considered, selected by the board's priority order
#   (indices first, then alphabetical) — you do not choose WHICH.
#   The operator BLOCKLIST chooses WHICH, by name, at runtime, with no restart.
BOX_MAX_UNDERLYINGS=1
BOX_STRIKE_LEVEL=1                        # ATM ±1 — the smallest candidate set
BOX_ENABLE_SHORT_BOX=false                # long boxes only for a first live run

# THE BLOCKLIST IS NOT AN ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE. It is durable operator state in
# `box_excluded_underlyings` (migration 012), managed at runtime:
#
#   GET    /api/box/excluded-underlyings
#   POST   /api/box/excluded-underlyings          {"symbol":"NIFTY","reason":"ban period"}
#   DELETE /api/box/excluded-underlyings/NIFTY
#
# It refuses ENTRY only, in every execution mode, at four independent chokepoints — two of which
# (BoxExecutionSimulator and CentralBoxExecutionGateway) cannot be disabled by configuration. It
# NEVER blocks an exit, a reduction, a protective cancel or a flatten, so excluding a name whose box
# is already open cannot trap the position.
#
# PRE-FLIGHT: confirm `GET /api/box/status` reports `excluded_underlyings.readable: true`. If it is
# false the durable list could not be READ, and the engine is refusing ALL new entry with the
# readiness blocker `underlying_exclusions_unreadable` — which is the correct behaviour (an
# unreadable blocklist cannot confirm any name is tradable) but will look like "nothing is trading".


# ── 6. ARMING ORDER ON THE DAY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every step is a deliberate, separate action. Nothing below is automatic.
#
#   1. Apply migrations. Confirm 012 is applied.
#   2. Boot. Confirm GET /api/box/status:
#        execution_mode: "live", db_enabled: true,
#        excluded_underlyings.readable: true,
#        reconciliation_complete: true, unknown_orders: 0,
#        operational_readiness.blockers — read EVERY entry-scoped blocker and clear it.
#   3. Set the blocklist for the day (POST any names you do not want risk on).
#   4. Confirm the broker session is the account you expect. A re-login to a DIFFERENT account
#      under the same API key is exactly what migration 011's `broker_account` binding exists to
#      catch — but note that an order-update frame carrying NO account still fails OPEN.
#   5. Arm live orders, then emergency flatten, then entry — in that order, so the panic button is
#      armed before anything can be submitted.
#   6. Arm the session ONE-SHOT (max_completed_trades: 1, max_entry_attempts: 1).
#   7. RUN the scanner.
#   8. Watch. Have cancel-working and flatten ready (WARNING 3).
#
# ── 7. AFTER IT GOES FLAT — WHAT DOES *NOT* HAPPEN ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# There is no auto-stop in this build. When the single box reaches FLAT:
#   - the session record stays ARMED, reporting state COMPLETED with remaining_trades: 0;
#   - further entry is refused by the spent budget (reason `session_limit_reached`), NOT by
#     disarming;
#   - the SCANNER KEEPS RUNNING and keeps publishing opportunities, each refused at the coordinator;
#   - the feed is NOT released while the scanner runs.
#
# So "it stops everything and shows me the numbers" is an OPERATOR sequence today:
#   POST /api/box/stop                     stop discovering
#   POST /api/box/session/disarm           make the refusal explicit rather than budget-implicit
#   GET  /api/box/trades/history?scope=today    the numbers: fills, fees, gross/net P&L, per-leg
#   GET  /api/box/events?limit=200              the decision trail (ENTRY, EXIT_TRIGGERED, EXIT …)
#   GET  /api/box/execution-attempts            anything that was attempted and aborted
#
# Note: `exit_charge_reconciliation` starts as `pending`. Charges read immediately after flat are
# LOCAL estimates; the broker-verified figures land asynchronously. Wait for that field to settle
# before treating the fee numbers as final.
